I ran into this site, trying to figure out what the heck
it meant that Rakshasa "have their hands on backwards".

http://www.silverblades-suitcase.com/adnd/index.html

[It turned out not to help, in this case, the detail
level was too low.]

Check out the part "Silverblade's AD&D Miniatures";
if nothing else, this person's painting skills need
admiring.

Looking at the characters, I see immediate YANIs for
character classes "Damsel" and "Hobbit Burgler"; it
still annoys me that NetHack's thief character class
doesn't _steal_ anything, and more female-specific
character classes couldn't hurt. A damsel, of course,
would be an expert at beguiling, charming, taming,
and such, while a Hobbit Burglar could clean out a
shop or a Zoo without waking the proprietor/inhabitants.

There's lots more to admire in there, and a general
comment on the art: if the graphic version of
NetHack popped up images like these of the
characters with which you are in combat (still
images next to the usual playfield would be fine,
much as "Bard's Tale I" did), you wouldn't find a
lot of six year olds playing NetHack any more.

I especially, though, loved the xorn.

